

Implementation Plan for Channels
The implementation is divided into three main areas: the object system (the channel itself),
the compiler (for syntax), and the VM runtime (the scheduler that manages blocked cores).

1. Object System: ChannelObject
In the vm/objects package, you should introduce a new ChannelObject type that implements IObject.

Internal Data Structure: This object would be a reference type.
The actual channel state would be in a shared struct that all channel IObjects point to. This struct would contain:

buffer: A FIFO queue (e.g. []IObject or list.List) to store values.

capacity: The buffer capacity (0 for unbuffered channels).

sendQueue: A queue of Cores waiting to send (ch <- val).

recvQueue: A queue of Cores waiting to receive (<- ch).


2. Compiler: New Syntax and Opcodes
The compiler in native/compiler should be extended to recognize channel syntax.

Creation: Recognize make(chan Type, capacity) and emit a new opcode, for example OpMakeChan.

Operations: Recognize the <- operator:

Send (ch <- value): Would emit an OpChanSend.

Receive (<-ch or val := <-ch): Would emit an OpChanRecv.

3. Sequencer and Opcodes: The Execution Logic
The new opcodes in vm/sequencers/native would implement the main logic:

OpMakeChan: Executed once, creates the ChannelObject and its shared internal data structure and puts it on the stack.

OpChanSend:

Extracts the channel and value from the stack.

Send logic:

If there's a Core waiting in recvQueue, sends the value directly to that core and "wakes" it (puts it back in runnable state).

Otherwise, if buffer isn't full, adds the value to the buffer.

Otherwise (buffer full), adds the current Core to sendQueue and puts the core in standby.

OpChanRecv:

Extracts the channel from the stack.

Receive logic:

If there's a Core waiting in sendQueue, receives the value directly from that core and wakes it.

Otherwise, if buffer isn't empty, extracts a value from the buffer.

Otherwise (buffer empty), adds the current Core to recvQueue and puts the core in standby.

4. Modifying the VM Scheduler (the Core Mechanism)
This is the most critical and interesting part, which integrates perfectly with your architecture. The execution loop in vm/handler/vm.go needs to be enhanced to handle cores in standby.

Core State: A Core needs a state. You could add a status field to Core with values like:

StatusReady (ready to be executed)

StatusBlocked (waiting on a channel)

StatusHalted (terminated)

Blocking Mechanism:

When an OpChanSend or OpChanRecv needs to put the core in standby, it can't just stop. It must signal to the scheduler that it's blocked.

The cleanest solution is for the opcode to call a new function on the IVMFullAccess interface, like BlockCurrentCore().

This function sets the Core's state to StatusBlocked and makes the Core.Execute() method return a special code to notify the scheduler it's blocked.

New Scheduler Loop:
The loop in VM.exec would change slightly. Instead of just having coresRunning and coresFree, you'd have:

coresReady: A queue of cores ready for execution.

coresBlocked: A map or list of cores in standby.

coresFree: The available cores.

The main loop:

Takes a core from the coresReady queue.

Executes it (core.Execute()).

If execution returns "blocked", VM moves the core to coresBlocked.

If execution terminates, VM moves the core to coresFree.

Otherwise, puts it back in the coresReady queue for next cycle (cooperative multitasking).

Unblocking Mechanism:

When an OpChanSend wakes a Core that was waiting to receive (and vice versa), the operation must notify the scheduler.

The scheduler will move the unblocked Core from coresBlocked to coresReady, making it eligible for execution again.
